\\,n+2-a\\,}.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-a}{n+2-a}\\,a+\\frac{1}{n+2-a}\n   =\\frac{(n+1-a)a+1}{n+2-a}\n   =\\frac{(n+1)a-a^{2}+1}{n+2-a}\n   =b,\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from the relation \\(a+b=n+1-(i-1)=n+2-a\\) that holds for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\). The \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) therefore satisfy both requirements of the problem, giving a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations with fewer sunny lines  \n\nThe three forbidden directions can be used to replace up to three of the lines \\(L_i\\) while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) keep the sunny line \\(L_i\\) defined in (2).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(H\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the index \\(i=n+1-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); by the argument of Section 2 the point lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n\nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line (\\(H\\)) is non‑sunny, giving  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Exactly two non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nUse the horizontal and vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) keep the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nPoints with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(H\\); points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(V\\).  \nIf \\(a,b\\ge2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and again \\(i=n+1-a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), so the point lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n\nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and precisely the two lines \\(H,V\\) are non‑sunny, yielding  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Exactly three non‑sunny lines (\\(k=n-3\\))\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad D:\\; x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) keep the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nPoints with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(H\\); points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(V\\); points with \\(a+b=2\\) lie on \\(D\\).  \nAll remaining points satisfy \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); as before \\(i=n+1-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and the point lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly the three lines \\(H,V,D\\) are non‑sunny, giving  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3 .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-3\\).  \nThe explicit constructions (3)–(6) show that each integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable. Therefore these four numbers are precisely the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
