y line and no two of the \\(S_i\\) coincide.\n\nNow remove the line \\(L_{n+1}\\) from \\(\\mathcal{C}_0\\) and replace it by the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  The resulting family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}_k=\\bigl\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nLet \\(s=a+b\\).  \n\n*If \\(s\\neq n+1\\):* the point lies on the line \\(L_s\\), which is still present\nin \\(\\mathcal{C}_k\\); hence it is covered.\n\n*If \\(s=n+1\\):* then \\((a,b)=(i,n+1-i)\\) for the unique integer\n\\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  By construction this point belongs to the sunny line\n\\(S_i\\).  Thus it is also covered.\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal{C}_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) all have slope \\(-1\\) and distinct intercepts,\nhence are pairwise distinct.  \nEach sunny line \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(m_i\\neq -1\\); consequently no \\(S_i\\) can\ncoincide with any \\(L_s\\).  Moreover the slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct,\nso the \\(S_i\\) are distinct among themselves.  Hence \\(\\mathcal{C}_k\\) consists\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because the\ntotal number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
