            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be an integer and let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{L_i : x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{D:x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n1. **Distinctness.** The vertical lines \\(L_i\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so they are pairwise distinct. The line \\(D\\) is not vertical, hence it is distinct from each \\(L_i\\). Thus \\(|\\mathcal L_n|=n\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny property.**  \n   - Each \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n   - The line \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   By definition a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) is *not* sunny. Hence none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **Covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n   - **Case \\(a\\le n-1\\).**  \n     The point satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\); therefore it lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n   - **Case \\(a=n\\).**  \n     The inequality \\(n+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\). The point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\), so it lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   In either case the point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct, non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n### Consequence\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the integer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nis admissible.\n\n---\n\n### Remarks on the incompleteness of the previous answer\n\nThe earlier solution asserted that \\(k=0\\) is the *only* possible value. To justify such a claim one would need to prove a converse statement: **no** collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can contain a sunny line. The present work does **not** provide such a non‑existence proof; consequently the possibility that configurations with one or more sunny lines exist remains open. Without either a construction for \\(k\\ge 1\\) or a proof of impossibility, the full set of admissible \\(k\\) cannot be determined. Hence the solution is partial.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
