 of points covered by a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   - A vertical line \\(x=i\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\) (resp. \\(n+1-j\\)) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   - The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   - Hence any non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, and the vertical/horizontal lines together can cover at most \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points, which is exactly \\(|S_n|\\).\n\n5. **Counting argument.**  \n   - Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \n   - Let the sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \n   - By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, so together they cover at most  \n     \\[\n     k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le k\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n     \\]  \n   - The \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can together cover at most \\(|S_n|= \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points (the vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines achieve this bound, any other non\u2011sunny line covers fewer points).  \n   - Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal F\\) is at most  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+k\\frac{n-1}{2}\n     =\\frac{(n-1)(n+k)}{2}.\n     \\]  \n   - Since every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\le\\frac{(n-1)(n+k)}{2},\n     \\]\n     which is always true, but we must also respect the *distinctness* of the lines.  \n   - If any sunny line is present, at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines must be omitted (otherwise we would have more than \\(n\\) lines). Removing a non\u2011sunny line reduces the maximal possible coverage by at least one point, because each non\u2011sunny line of the optimal vertical/horizontal/diagonal family contributes a *unique* point that no other line of that family contains (the point \\((i, n+1-i)\\) lies only on the diagonal, while the point \\((i,1)\\) lies only on the vertical line \\(x=i\\), etc.).  \n   - Consequently, with \\(k\\ge1\\) the total number of distinct points that can be covered by exactly \\(n\\) lines is **strictly less** than \\(|S_n|\\), a contradiction.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The contradiction shows that no configuration with a sunny line exists. Hence every admissible family must consist solely of non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \n   - Together with the explicit construction of \u00a72, we have proved that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\bigl\\{L_i:x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{D:x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.* The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, and the diagonal line \\(D\\) is not vertical; hence \\(|\\mathcal L_n|=n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny.* Each \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\). By definition none of them is sunny.\n\n*Covering.* Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) and therefore lies on \\(L_a\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then the inequality \\(n+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\). Consequently a family with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line cannot contain many points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\neq0,\\;\\alpha\\neq\\beta .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(d=\\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)\\) and write \\(\\alpha=d\\alpha',\\;\\beta=d\\beta'\\) with \\(\\gcd(\\alpha',\\beta')=1\\).\nAll integer solutions of (2) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=\\bigl(x_0+t\\beta',\\;y_0-t\\alpha'\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution.\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha'\\neq\\beta'\\) and both are non\u2011zero, the step vector \\((\\beta',-\\alpha')\\) has non\u2011zero components and is not of the form \\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nInside the triangular region defined by \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\) the sequence (3) can therefore intersect at most every second integer lattice line parallel to the sides of the triangle. A direct inspection shows that the length of the intersection of the arithmetic progression (3) with the triangle is bounded by the smallest distance between two parallel sides, i.e. by \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).\n\nHence we have\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }\\ell .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Maximal coverage of a non\u2011sunny line\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(i,b)\\in S_n\\}\\,|=n+1-i. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=j\\) is analogous.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\); thus it contains \\(n-1\\) points.\n\nConsequently any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and the family consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the diagonal line covers **exactly** all \\(|S_n|\\) points, as shown in Section\u202f1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of a sunny line in a covering family\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{m_1,\\dots ,m_{n-k}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny and the \\(m_j\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\nFrom (4) each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, so the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le k\\frac{n-1}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can together cover at most \\(|S_n|\\) points, because the vertical/horizontal/diagonal family already attains the absolute maximum \\(|S_n|\\) (Section\u202f3) and any other choice can only decrease the total. Hence the total number of points covered by \\(\\mathcal F\\) is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|+k\\frac{n-1}{2}\n= \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+k\\frac{n-1}{2}\n= \\frac{(n-1)(n+k)}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge1\\), then at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines of the optimal vertical/horizontal/diagonal family must be omitted (otherwise we would have more than \\(n\\) lines). Each omitted non\u2011sunny line removes **at least one** point that is not covered by any other line of that optimal family:  \n\n* the diagonal line supplies the unique points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\);  \n* the vertical line \\(x=i\\) supplies the unique point \\((i,1)\\) that does not lie on any other vertical line nor on the diagonal.\n\nTherefore, after removing any non\u2011sunny line, the maximal possible coverage drops by at least one, giving an upper bound strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\). Adding the contribution of the sunny lines (which is at most the quantity in (6)) cannot compensate this loss, because (6) is at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) per sunny line, while the loss is at least one per omitted non\u2011sunny line, and we have omitted exactly \\(k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\)). Consequently the total number of distinct points that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal F\\) is **strictly less** than \\(|S_n|\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nHence no such family with \\(k\\ge1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThe impossibility argument of Section\u202f4 shows that \\(k\\ge1\\) cannot occur.  \n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is solved: the only possible number of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the given conditions is \\(k=0\\).**"

