athbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAmong all such lines the one that can meet the most points of \\(S_n\\) is the line of slope \\(1\\), namely \\(x-y=0\\).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(a=b\\) and \\(2a\\le n+1\\); hence the number of them equals  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nAny other sunny line has a different slope, and the integer points of \\(S_n\\) lying on it form a subset of the above set, so the same bound applies.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{for every sunny line }\\ell}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  How many points can a non‑sunny line contain?\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) contains the points \\((i,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(i,b)\\in S_n\\}\\,|=n+1-i\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe maximal value \\(n\\) is attained for \\(i=1\\).  \n*Horizontal lines* are analogous.  \n\nThe *diagonal* line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\); it therefore contains \\(n-1\\) points.\n\nHence every non‑sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of the vertical lines\n\nFix \\(a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and consider the set  \n\n\\[\nV_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\subset S_n .\n\\]\n\nThe only non‑sunny line that contains any point of \\(V_a\\) is the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the diagonal meets \\(V_a\\) only at the single point \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect \\(V_a\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(V_a\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, which would force the line to be vertical.\n\nBy (4) a sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points to \\(V_a\\), while \\(|V_a|=n+1-a\\ge n/2+1\\) for every \\(a\\le